tar_load(n.p53.ctnnb1)
tar_load(spearman.cor)
sp.cor <- round(spearman.cor, 2)
ue.hyp <- num_hypermut('ue', stab3, stab4, mfest.purity.g20)
ue.hyp2 <- y.of.n(ue.hyp, mfest.purity.g20, 'ue')
oe.hyp <- num_hypermut('oe', stab3, stab4, mfest.purity.g20)
oe.hyp2 <- y.of.n(oe.hyp, mfest.purity.g20, 'oe')
@
\noindent\textbf{Ovarian endometrioid and uterine endometrioid carcinomas}\\
We employed a highly sensitive mutation analysis to identify somatic alterations in ovarian endometrioid (n=\Sexpr{downstream$n.types2['OE']}) and uterine endometrioid (n=\Sexpr{downstream$n.types2['UE']}) cancers (Supplementary Tables \ref{tab:mutationswes} and \ref{tab:mutationswgs}).
The median number of somatic alterations was \Sexpr{round(mut.oall['OE'], 0)} for both ovarian endometrioid and uterine endometrioid cancers and did not appear to be associated with tumor purity (Spearman correlation = \Sexpr{sp.cor}, Supplementary Fig. \ref{fig:sfig1}, Supplementary Tables \ref{tab:sequencing},  \ref{tab:mutationswes}, and \ref{tab:mutationswgs}).
Consistent with previous analyses, we observed a bimodal distribution of the number of mutations for uterine endometrioid carcinomas, with nearly half of the \Sexpr{downstream$n.types2['UE']} tumors analyzed having more than 500 mutations per exome, likely the result of a hypermutator status \cite{cancergenomeatlasresearchnetwork2013} (Supplementary Fig.\ref{fig:sfig1}).
As in previous studies, uterine endometrioid carcinomas were more likely to be hypermutated
(\Sexpr{ue.hyp2} patients) compared to ovarian endometrioid carcinomas (\Sexpr{oe.hyp2} patients).

Evaluation of the altered genes in ovarian endometrioid and uterine endometrioid carcinomas without a hypermutation phenotype revealed similar affected pathways (Fig. \ref{fig:fig2}).
Mutations involving chromatin regulating genes, and PI3K and RAS pathways were common in both tumor types.
The proportion of patients with alterations in the \textit{PTEN, PIK3CA, KRAS, PIK3R1, NF1,} or \textit{ARID1A} genes was {\Sexpr{prop['OE']}}\% for ovarian endometrioid and {\Sexpr{prop['UE']}}\% for uterine endometrioid, consistent with previous studies \cite{legallo2014, talhouk2015, hollis2020}.
As previously reported \cite{mcconechy2014}, we observed mutations in \textit{TP53} and \textit{CTNNB1} in \Sexpr{n.p53.ctnnb1['OE']} patients with ovarian endometrioid carcinomas ({\Sexpr{pctfun(n.p53.ctnnb1['OE'], mfest.purity.g20, 'oe')}}\% of patients) and \Sexpr{n.p53.ctnnb1['UE']} patients with uterine endometrioid carcinomas ({\Sexpr{pctfun(n.p53.ctnnb1['UE'], mfest.purity.g20, 'ue')}}\% of patients).
We identified several mutations in driver genes among patients with ovarian endometrioid carcinomas that have not been previously reported, including the homologous recombination \textit{RAD51C} gene, the Notch signaling genes (\textit{NOTCH1-4}), chromatin remodeling genes \textit{SMARCA1/4}, transcription factor \textit{E2F1}, the receptor tyrosine kinase \textit{RET}, and the \textit{ABL1} kinase.


<<ch04>>=
tar_load(n.esr1)
tar_load(n.hotspot)
@

Interestingly, we found that \Sexpr{n.esr1} of the \Sexpr{downstream$types2["uterine endometrioid"]} patients with uterine endometrioid carcinomas had alterations in \textit{ESR1}\label{esr1}, including \Sexpr{n.hotspot} hotspot mutations at Tyr537 (Y537C and Y537N) and an alteration at Leu536 (L536R) (Supplementary Fig. \ref{fig:sfig2}).
The Tyr537 hotspot constitutively activates the estrogen receptor independent of estrogen, and has been reported in patients with breast cancer that have become resistant to tamoxifen or aromatase inhibitor therapy \cite{toy2013, robinson2013}.
While the consequence of the Leu536 amino acid substitution is not well understood, its adjacency to the Tyr537 hotspot suggests that a similar functional role is likely.
Though neither woman with an \textit{ESR1} hotspot mutation had been treated with anti-estrogen therapy, both women were above the average age of menopause and would likely have had low levels of estrogen.

To understand the role of mutations in these ovarian cancer subtypes in the context of mutational signatures \cite{alexandrov2020}, we evaluated the types of mutations and linked these to mutational processes that include exogenous or endogenous mutagen exposure, enzymatic modification of DNA, and defective DNA repair (Supplementary Fig. \ref{fig:sfig3}a).
As expected, we found that the age-related mutation signature, Signature 1, characterized by a predominance of C$>$T transitions was present in all samples.
Such C$>$T transitions are believed to be caused by spontaneous deamination of 5-methylcytosine.
In patients with hypermutator defects, we identified signatures 6 and 15 known to be associated with DNA mismatch repair deficiency and microsatellite instability \cite{alexandrov2020}.

In addition to mutations, we evaluated both whole exome and genome sequence data for focal amplifications. 
For WGS data, we examined the read-pair information to pinpoint the presence of amplicons that may be linked genomically using the Trellis approach \cite{papp2018} (Fig. \ref{fig:fig3}a, Supplementary Fig. \ref{fig:sfig4}-\ref{fig:sfig7}, and Supplementary Table \ref{tab:ampwgs}). 
The multiple genomic insertion sites as a result of \textit{MYC} and \textit{KRAS} amplification in tumors from patients CGOV161 and CGOV172, respectively, could be connected to other amplicons (Fig. \ref{fig:fig3}b), likely indicating that these genes and the neighboring genomic region at an insertion site were duplicated in successive cell divisions \cite{bignell2007}. 
\textit{KRAS} has been shown to be amplified in ovarian endometrioid and HGSOC and has been associated with an aggressive phenotype of metastatic uterine endometrioid carcinoma \cite{rahman2013, birkeland2012}, while \textit{MYC} amplifications have been observed in a variety of ovarian cancers including endometrioid carcinomas \cite{jung2018}.

We evaluated both whole exome and genome sequence data for homozygous deletions and identified losses in multiple genes known to be altered in ovarian endometrioid carcinomas, including \textit{PTEN}, \textit{NF1}, \textit{ARID1A}, \textit{BRCA1}, and others. 
Additionally, we identified deletions in genes \textit{TSC2} and \textit{STK11} that have not been previously reported in endometrioid ovarian carcinoma (Supplementary Tables \ref{tab:cnvwes} and \ref{tab:deletionswgs}).
\textit{TSC2} deletions have been observed in HGSOC and mutations in this gene have been associated with increased sensitivity to rapamycin-induced apoptosis, making \textit{TSC2} a potentially targetable biomarker \cite{tanwar2014, guertin2005}.
Germline \textit{STK11} inactivating mutations have been associated with Peutz-Jeghers syndrome \cite{schumacher2005}, and somatic changes in this gene have been reported in a variety of cancers including non-small cell lung cancer, cervical cancer, colorectal cancer, melanoma, and pancreatic cancer with predominantly truncating mutations, but have not yet been reported in endometrioid ovarian carcinoma.

For samples with available WGS, we also used Trellis to identify rearrangements and fusions (Supplementary Fig. \ref{fig:sfig4}, Supplementary Table \ref{tab:fusionswgs}). 
These analyses revealed several novel rearrangements that were predicted to result in in-frame protein fusions in ovarian endometrioid carcinomas including \textit{ATAD1-PTEN}, the neurofibromin genes \textit{NF1} and \textit{NF2}, as well as cyclin-dependent and polo-like kinase genes \textit{CDK7} and \textit{PLK5} (Fig. \ref{fig:fig3}c).
The \textit{ATAD1-PTEN} rearrangement was linked to the homozygous deletion of a portion of \textit{PTEN}.
While \textit{PTEN} loss in the ovarian surface epithelium has been shown to induce papillary serous ovarian cancer, rearrangements of \textit{PTEN} associated with deletions have not been previously reported in ovarian endometrioid tumors \cite{tanwar2014}.
The neurofibromin gene fusions \textit{NF1-RAB11FIP4} and \textit{UBXN6-NF2} were identified in patient CGOV161T with ovarian endometrioid carinoma.
Rearrangements involving \textit{NF1} and \textit{NF2} have been reported in other cancers and have been associated with therapeutic resistance in HGSOC \cite{philpott2017, papp2018}, but have not been previously identified in ovarian endometrioid carcinoma.
Fusions of kinase genes \textit{IPO11-CDK7} and \textit{PLK5-TCF3} were detected in two different tumors.
\textit{CDK7} is involved in cell cycle progression and \textit{CDK7} inhibitors have been shown to exert broad cytotoxicity against ovarian tumors \cite{zhang2017, zeng2018}.
The \textit{IPO11} gene is involved in nuclear protein import and is expressed in both ovarian and endometrial cells.
The fusion of \textit{IPO11-CDK7} may lead to constitutive activation of \textit{CDK7}, promoting cell cycle progression and tumor growth.
The polo-like kinase 5 gene (\textit{PLK5}) has been reported to be altered in glioblastoma and, along with other members of the PLK family, plays a role in cell division and centriole duplication \cite{decarcer2011, moyer2019}.
As recent studies suggest that aberrant expression of PLKs affect cell division and influence genomic instability \cite{habedanck2005,kuriyama2009}, PLKs may provide a useful target for therapeutic interventions \cite{press2019}.


\vspace{1em}
<<ch05>>=
tar_load(nmut)
tar_load(crc.tumor)
tar_load(ov.tumor)
@

\noindent\textbf{Ovarian mucinous and GI mucinous cancers}\\
We analyzed \Sexpr{downstream$types2["ovarian mucinous"]} patients with ovarian mucinous carcinoma and \Sexpr{downstream$types2["colorectal"]} patients with colorectal mucinous carcinomas for sequence (mutations and mutation signatures) and structural alterations (deletions, amplifications, rearrangements, and fusions).
Using a threshold of 500 mutations per exome, we identified \Sexpr{nrow(nmut)} hypermutated mucinous tumors. 
One was mucinous colorectal carcinoma \Sexpr{crc.tumor$lab_id} which had \Sexpr{prettyNum(max(crc.tumor$n), big.mark=",")} exome mutations, likely a consequence of the R197S mutation in \textit{POLE} (Fig. \ref{fig:fig4} and Supplementary Tables \ref{tab:mutationswes} and \ref{tab:mutationswgs}).
The hotspot somatic mutation observed in \textit{POLE} occurred in the exonuclease domain of the encoded protein and has been reported in a small subset of microsatellite‐stable and hypermutated colorectal carcinomas, affecting the proofreading activity of the enzyme during DNA replication \cite{guerra2017}.
The other hypermutated mucinous carcinoma was ovarian tumor \Sexpr{ov.tumor$lab_id} with \Sexpr{prettyNum(max(ov.tumor$n), big.mark=",")} exome mutations.
Using the Open Custom Ranked Analysis of Variants Toolkit we determined that this tumor had at least two variants implicated in mismatch repair deficiency, T981M in \textit{MSH3} which is predicted to disrupt protein function according to SIFT, as well as a R248Tfs*8 in \textit{MSH6} which is predicted as pathogenic/likely pathogenic by ClinVar \cite{sim2012, landrum2014, pagel2020}.

Of the genes known to be altered through sequence changes in ovarian and colorectal mucinous carcinomas, we found alterations at similar frequencies in \textit{KRAS}, \textit{PIK3CA}, \textit{RNF43}, and \textit{TP53}.
We identified several genes known to be mutated in ovarian mucinous carcinomas including \textit{ERBB2, ARID1A}, and \textit{CREBBP} \cite{cheasley2019, ricci2018, lin2011a, anglesio2013}.
We also detected mutations in the chromatin modifier gene \textit{SMARCA4} that had not been previously reported in ovarian mucinous carcinomas.
With the exception of the \textit{POLE} mutant tumor, these genes were infrequently altered in colorectal mucinous carcinomas.

<<cnv>>=
tar_load(erbb2.n.amp)
tar_load(cdkn2a)
tar_load(n.cdkn2.mut)
tar_load(ncrc2)
tar_load(crc.frac)
tar_load(ci3)
@

Analyses of focal copy number changes revealed amplifications of \textit{CCND1} and \textit{ERBB2}, and homozygous deletions of \textit{CDKN2A/B} in mucinous ovarian carcinomas, a finding consistent with previous studies \cite{papp2018,dimova2004,niederacher1999,ryland2013}.
Amplification of \textit{ERBB2} was observed in \Sexpr{erbb2.n.amp} of the \Sexpr{downstream$types2["ovarian mucinous"]} ovarian mucinous carcinomas and provides a potentially useful target for trastuzumab therapy \cite{mcalpine2009}.
\textit{CDKN2A} deletions were observed in \Sexpr{cdkn2a$n[cdkn2a$alteration=="deletion"]} cases, and \Sexpr{n.cdkn2.mut} additional tumors had a sequence alteration in this gene \cite{niederacher1999}.
In contrast, \Sexpr{ncrc2} of the colorectal mucinous carcinomas had copy number alternations in \textit{ERBB2}, \textit{CCND1} or \textit{CDKN2A/B} (\Sexpr{crc.frac}, 95\% CI: \Sexpr{ci3}).

Interestingly, homozygous deletions of \textit{NF1} were present in a small number of both ovarian and colorectal mucinous carcinomas.
In addition, we identified a novel \textit{NF1-RAD51D} rearrangement resulting in the likely inactivation of \textit{NF1} in one ovarian mucinous carcinoma (Fig. \ref{fig:fig4}).
Inactivating alterations of \textit{NF1} have been shown to lead to RAS overactivity in HGSOC \cite{cancergenomeatlasresearchnetwork2011}.
Although the role of \textit{RAD51D} is unclear in this context, germline \textit{RAD51D} carriers have been reported to develop ovarian tumors at an early age  \cite{sanchez-bermudez2018}.
We also identified a novel \textit{MED1-STAT5B} rearrangement in the same ovarian mucinous carcinoma (Supplementary Fig. \ref{fig:sfig8}).
\textit{MED1} alterations have been implicated in bladder and breast cancers and may be a potential target for treatment in endocrine resistant breast cancer \cite{cui2012, viallefont2001a}.

