\textbf{(a)} Circos plots for linked amplicons for individual tumor samples with $>$100 structural variants.
Circos plots depict copy number alterations as well as intra- and inter-chromosomal rearrangements.
Segmented normalized estimates of read depth were used to identify candidate focal amplifications (yellow) and focal deletions (green).
Rearranged read pairs and split reads were used to identify inter- and intra-chromosomal rearrangements (blue).
\textbf{(b)} For linked amplicon graphs, nodes represent genes, node size represents number of times the segment is represented in the genome, color indicates the chromosome location of the amplified segment, and edges denote segments that are connected in the rearranged cancer genome.
Known driver genes indicated by triangles are often hubs in these networks, suggesting that amplification of these genes occurs early and can be connected to many of the other amplicons.
\textbf{(c)} Reads aligned to the fusion junction of two representative rearrangements.
Split reads that span the fusion junction are shown in black, while read pairs, that reside on either side of the junction are shown in green and blue, providing additional evidence for specific rearrangements.}
  \label{fig:fig3}
\end{figure}


\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../docs/figure/figure4.Rmd/fig4-1.pdf}
  \caption{\textbf{Integrated genomic analyses of ovarian and colorectal mucinous cancers.}
    Integrated analyses of somatic point mutations, structural variants including linked amplicons, deletions, and rearrangements, organized by pathways.
    The top of the figure shows number of each type of alteration, the middle portion reflects the alterations present in each sample for each gene and pathway, and the bottom shows the mutation spectra. }
  \label{fig:fig4}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../docs/figure/figure5.Rmd/fig5-1.pdf}
  \caption{\textbf{Comparison of mutation frequencies across histological subtypes in commonly altered genes.}
    \textbf{(a)} For known cancer driver genes with an alteration frequency of at least 10\% in patients with ovarian or uterine endometrioid cancer, we compared the proportion of samples with alterations between these histological subtypes.
  Overall, mutation rates were comparable in ovarian and uterine endometrioid subtypes with 90\% CIs of the log odds ratio including zero (no difference).
  By contrast, we observed more differences between GI mucinous and ovarian mucinous tumors despite only 6 driver genes having an overall mutation frequency of at least 10\% \textbf{(b)}.
  Higher mutation rates were observed in ovarian mucinous tumors at \textit{CDKN2A}, \textit{KRAS}, \textit{ERBB2}, and \textit{TP53}.
  For all panels, horizontal line segments indicate 90\% CIs and filled circles denote posterior medians.  Hypermutators were excluded from these analyses.}
  \label{fig:fig5}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../docs/figure/figure6.Rmd/fig6-1.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Ovarian endometrioid carcinomas are epigenetically similar to healthy uterine endometrium while ovarian mucinous carcinomas are mixed with both mucinous- and endometrial-like signatures.}
  \textbf{(a)}
  We trained a linear discriminant model for the classification of samples as stomach mucinous, colorectal mucinous, or uterine endometrial from variably methylated CpG sites in TCGA samples.
    Ellipses denote the 95${^\text{th}}$ percentile of the bivariate normal distribution for each of these histology classifications.
    \textbf{(b)} Using the linear discriminant model from \textbf{(a)}, the histological classification  of samples were obtained and overlaid on the ellipses re-drawn from panel \textbf{(a)}.
    The co-clustering of colorectal and stomach mucinous carcinomas with the corresponding tumor types from TCGA confirm that the methylation patterns that separate these tissues in TCGA broadly reflect histology differences (top panels of \textbf{(b)}).
  While ovarian mucinous and ovarian endometrioid samples have no counterpart in TCGA (bottom panels of \textbf{(b)}), we evaluated whether these samples would be epigenetically similar to their morphological counterparts in TCGA.
  \textbf{(c)}
  Class probabilities from the linear discriminant model evaluated on the analyzed samples.
  As expected, patients with stomach and colorectal mucinous carcinomas were correctly classified as mucinous, and patients with ovarian endometrioid carcinomas were correctly classified as endometrial.
  Interestingly, classification of patients with ovarian mucinous carcinoma was more heterogenous, with nearly half of the samples exhibiting a mucinous signature and the remaining samples bearing more of a resemblance to endometrial methylation patterns. The two colorectal mucinous samples in panel \textbf{(b)} (top-left) were sequencing replicates from a single patient's tumor (patient CGCRC330).}
    \label{fig:fig6}
\end{figure}

\end{spacing}

